james-will-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps james-will-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders" is true. Is S2, "As an experimenting body, it cannot be said to have completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders.", true then?
james-will-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: james-will-4.logic.r2 has "subjects_few" and "work_time" and "subjects_few ∧ work_time → ¬exp_fulfilled"; james-will-4.logic.r2.2 and james-will-4.logic.r2.3 have "subjects_few" and "work_time"; they differ when only "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders", "the clairvoyant and other subjects who will allow themselves to be experimented upon are few and far between" and "work with such subjects takes an immense amount of time and has had to be carried on at odd intervals by members engaged in other pursuits" are true, which the second allows
james-will-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps james-will-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders", "the clairvoyant and other subjects who will allow themselves to be experimented upon are few and far between" and "work with such subjects takes an immense amount of time and has had to be carried on at odd intervals by members engaged in other pursuits" are true. Is S3, "The reasons for this lie in two circumstances: first, the clairvoyant and other subjects who will allow themselves to be experimented upon are few and far between; and, secondly, work with them takes an immense amount of time, and has had to be carried on at odd intervals by members engaged in other pursuits.", true then?
james-will-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: james-will-4.logic.r2 has "records_fallible → lit_worthless" and "weather_work ∧ lit_worthless → indispensable"; james-will-4.logic.r2.2 and james-will-4.logic.r2.3 have "subjects_few ∧ work_time → ¬exp_fulfilled" and "records_fallible → lit_worthless" and "lit_worthless ∧ weather_work → indispensable"; they differ when only "as a weather bureau accumulating reports of phenomena such as apparitions, the S. P. R. has done an immense amount of work", "as an experimenting body, the S. P. R. has completely fulfilled the hopes of its founders", "the clairvoyant and other subjects who will allow themselves to be experimented upon are few and far between", "work with such subjects takes an immense amount of time and has had to be carried on at odd intervals by members engaged in other pursuits", "the records of the facts cited in the literature of the supernatural are so fallible and imperfect that at most they suggest keeping a window open on that quarter", "the existing literature of the supernatural is practically worthless for evidential purposes" and "the S. P. R.'s function, even as nothing but a weather bureau catching sporadic apparitions in their freshness, is indispensable in the scientific organism" are true, which the first allows
